WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.170
CARTER MORGAN: This
week, we're talking

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:03.000
about encryption,
migrating legacy

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.478
workflows, and Kubernetes.

00:00:04.478 --> 00:00:07.020
I'm Carter Morgan, and welcome
to "This Week in Cloud," where

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.200
we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.420
Cloud computing is
certainly the new standard.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.510
But the complexity, cost,
and risk of a cloud migration

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.610
may be holding
your business back.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.860
Bare Metal Solution is
designed to bridge this gap

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.810
by offering hardware
that supports enterprise

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.812
applications with a dedicated
connection to Google Cloud.

00:00:24.812 --> 00:00:27.270
It's time for a cloud solution
that works for a specialized

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.400
legacy workloads.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.800
As part of our non-stop
push for security,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.882
we're updating the
external key manager,

00:00:33.882 --> 00:00:35.340
which allows
businesses to maintain

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.620
separation between
their encrypted data

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.600
and the keys of that encryption.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.330
We're also introducing
key access justifications

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.220
to provide a detailed
justification

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.980
each time an external
encryption key is requested.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.350
With these two services,
you are the ultimate arbiter

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.290
of your data.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:52.890
There's always more
work to be done.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.340
And scaling that work
is a constant challenge.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.740
But for Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.430
we've begun the preview of
Batch, a Cloud native solution

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:03.090
for running batch workloads at
scale in an optimized manner.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.430
Try it out and then let
us know what you think.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.020
Seriously, the
product team wants

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.600
you to email them your
thoughts and questions.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.640
And speaking of
Kubernetes, Cloud Code

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.140
is now generally available.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.340
Developers can write,
debug, and deploy code

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.020
to Google Cloud or any
Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.400
And if you miss the features
of your preferred IDE,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:21.767
there's an extension for that.

00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:23.850
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.070
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.800
Any thoughts or ideas, leave
us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.210
I'm Carter Morgan, and
we'll see you in the Cloud.

